The flow illustrates a **single write path** that always funnels through the engine, while **read paths** are dynamically resolved based on the current contract (strong reads hit the quorum of all regions; eventual reads may be served locally).

### Core Java domain model (LLD)

```java
// Consistency contract enumeration
public enum ConsistencyLevel {
    STRONG,          // quorum across all regions
    EVENTUAL,        // local read‑your‑writes only
    HYBRID           // strong for compliance‑critical entities, eventual otherwise
}

// Marker interface for all domain events
public interface DomainEvent {
    String getEntityId();
    long getVersion();
}

// Abstract base for write commands
public abstract class WriteCommand {
    private final String entityId;
    private final ConsistencyLevel consistency;

    protected WriteCommand(String entityId, ConsistencyLevel consistency) {
        this.entityId = entityId;
        this.consistency = consistency;
    }
    public String getEntityId() { return entityId; }
    public ConsistencyLevel getConsistency() { return consistency; }
}

// Concrete command example
public final class UpdateCustomerCommand extends WriteCommand {
    private final String newName;
    public UpdateCustomerCommand(String entityId, ConsistencyLevel consistency, String newName) {
        super(entityId, consistency);
        this.newName = newName;
    }
    public String getNewName() { return newName; }
}

// Event definition
public final class CustomerUpdatedEvent implements DomainEvent {
    private final String entityId;
    private final long version;
    private final String newName;
    public CustomerUpdatedEvent(String entityId, long version, String newName) {
        this.entityId = entityId;
        this.version = version;
        this.newName = newName;
    }
    public String getEntityId() { return entityId; }
    public long getVersion() { return version; }
    public String getNewName() { return newName; }
}

// Strategy interface for consistency handling
public interface ConsistencyStrategy {
    void apply(WriteCommand cmd);
    DomainEvent read(String entityId);
}

// Strong‑consistency implementation using Axon command bus + Resilience4j circuit breaker
public class StrongConsistencyStrategy implements ConsistencyStrategy {
    private final CommandGateway commandGateway;
    private final CircuitBreaker circuitBreaker;

    public StrongConsistencyStrategy(CommandGateway commandGateway, CircuitBreaker circuitBreaker) {
        this.commandGateway = commandGateway;
        this.circuitBreaker = circuitBreaker;
    }

    @Override
    public void apply(WriteCommand cmd) {
        Supplier<Void> supplier = () -> {
            commandGateway.sendAndWait(cmd);
            return null;
        };
        circuitBreaker.executeSupplier(supplier);
    }

    @Override
    public DomainEvent read(String entityId) {
        // Axon query side – always hits a quorum view
        return commandGateway.query("StrongRead", entityId, DomainEvent.class).join();
    }
}

// Eventual‑consistency implementation that publishes to Kafka
public class EventualConsistencyStrategy implements ConsistencyStrategy {
    private final KafkaTemplate<String, DomainEvent> kafkaTemplate;
    private final String topic = "entity-events";

    public EventualConsistencyStrategy(KafkaTemplate<String, DomainEvent> kafkaTemplate) {
        this.kafkaTemplate = kafkaTemplate;
    }

    @Override
    public void apply(WriteCommand cmd) {
        // Convert command to event and fire‑and‑forget
        DomainEvent event = new CustomerUpdatedEvent(
            cmd.getEntityId(),
            System.currentTimeMillis(),
            ((UpdateCustomerCommand) cmd).getNewName()
        );
        kafkaTemplate.send(topic, cmd.getEntityId(), event);
    }

    @Override
    public DomainEvent read(String entityId) {
        // Local cache read – eventual consistency
        // (implementation omitted for brevity)
        return null;
    }
}

// Facade that selects the appropriate strategy at runtime
public class ConsistencyFacade {
    private final Map<ConsistencyLevel, ConsistencyStrategy> strategies;

    public ConsistencyFacade(Map<ConsistencyLevel, ConsistencyStrategy> strategies) {
        this.strategies = strategies;
    }

    public void handle(WriteCommand cmd) {
        strategies.get(cmd.getConsistency()).apply(cmd);
    }

    public DomainEvent fetch(String entityId, ConsistencyLevel level) {
        return strategies.get(level).read(entityId);
    }
}
```

### Spring REST entry point

```java
@RestController
@RequestMapping("/api/v1/customers")
public class CustomerController {
    private final ConsistencyFacade facade;

    public CustomerController(ConsistencyFacade facade) {
        this.facade = facade;
    }

    @PostMapping("/{id}")
    public ResponseEntity<Void> update(
            @PathVariable String id,
            @RequestParam ConsistencyLevel level,
            @RequestBody UpdateCustomerPayload payload) {

        UpdateCustomerCommand cmd = new UpdateCustomerCommand(id, level, payload.getName());
        facade.handle(cmd);
        return ResponseEntity.accepted().build();
    }

    @GetMapping("/{id}")
    public ResponseEntity<CustomerDto> get(
            @PathVariable String id,
            @RequestParam ConsistencyLevel level) {

        DomainEvent event = facade.fetch(id, level);
        if (event == null) {
            return ResponseEntity.notFound().build();
        }
        CustomerDto dto = new CustomerDto(event.getEntityId(), ((CustomerUpdatedEvent) event).getNewName());
        return ResponseEntity.ok(dto);
    }
}
```

### Comparative table of strategy characteristics

| Dimension                | StrongConsistencyStrategy                              | EventualConsistencyStrategy                               |
|--------------------------|--------------------------------------------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------|
| Latency (write)          | 150‑250 ms (quorum round‑trip)                         | 20‑40 ms (fire‑and‑forget)                                 |
| Fault tolerance          | Requires ≥ N‑1 region availability                     | Degrades gracefully; each region can operate independently |
| Conflict resolution      | Axon event‑sourcing with deterministic replay           | Kafka consumer with CRDT‑style merge function              |
| Use‑case fit             | Financial transactions, regulatory audit trails        | Social feeds, recommendation updates                       |
| Observability footprint | Axon‑monitor + Resilience4j metrics                     | Kafka metrics + custom lag gauges                         |

### Adaptive decision engine

The engine continuously ingests two telemetry streams:

1. **Network health** – measured via Spring Cloud Sleuth spans and Resilience4j health indicators (latency, error‑rate, circuit‑breaker state).
2. **Workload classification** – a lightweight classifier (e.g., a rule‑based engine) tags incoming commands as *critical* or *best‑effort*.

A Spring `@Scheduled` task recomputes a **global consistency policy** every 5 seconds:

```java
@Component
public class PolicyReconciler {
    private final ConsistencyFacade facade;
    private final NetworkHealthMonitor monitor;
    private final WorkloadClassifier classifier;

    @Scheduled(fixedDelay = 5000)
    public void reconcile() {
        if (monitor.isRegionLinkDegraded() && classifier.isHighCriticalLoad()) {
            // force strong for all critical entities
            facade.setDefaultLevel(ConsistencyLevel.STRONG);
        } else if (monitor.isLowLatency()) {
            // allow hybrid blending
            facade.setDefaultLevel(ConsistencyLevel.HYBRID);
        } else {
            facade.setDefaultLevel(ConsistencyLevel.EVENTUAL);
        }
    }
}
```

When the policy changes, the `ConsistencyFacade` swaps its internal map, causing subsequent requests to follow the new path without any client‑side redeployment.

### Observability surface

All strategy implementations publish standardized `ConsistencyEvent` records to a dedicated **metrics topic**. Grafana dashboards consume these records to display:

- **Current policy distribution** (percentage of requests per level)
- **Regional lag** (Kafka consumer lag per replica)
- **Circuit‑breaker health** (open/half‑open/closed states)

Because the engine centralizes the contract, operators can enforce compliance (e.g., “all GDPR‑subject data must never drop below STRONG”) via a simple YAML rule:

```yaml
consistency:
  overrides:
    - entityPattern: "eu-.*"
      requiredLevel: STRONG
```

The configuration is loaded at startup by a `@ConfigurationProperties` bean and injected into the `PolicyReconciler`. This declarative approach eliminates ad‑hoc scripting and guarantees that policy changes are version‑controlled alongside the application code.

In sum, the Hybrid Consistency Architecture unifies **decision logic**, **messaging**, and **persistence** behind a clean Java domain model. By leveraging Resilience4j for fault‑aware circuit breaking, Axon for deterministic event sourcing, and Spring Kafka for low‑latency eventual propagation, the engine delivers adaptive guarantees while keeping the client API stable and the operational footprint minimal.

## ⚙️ Adaptive Consistency Engine (Mermaid Diagram)

The Adaptive Consistency Engine (ACE) is the heart of the hybrid architecture. It sits between the client-facing API gateway and the underlying persistence fabric, orchestrating consistency decisions, policy enforcement, and fault‑tolerant propagation. The diagram below visualises the end‑to‑end flow, highlighting the key responsibilities of each sub‑component and the data paths that bind them together.

```mermaid
graph TD
    client[Client] --> gateway[API Gateway]
    gateway --> engine[Adaptive Consistency Engine]
    engine --> decision[Decision Service]
    decision --> policy[Policy Reconciler]
    policy --> eventStore[Axon Event Store]
    eventStore --> kafka[Kafka Topic]
    kafka --> replica1[Replica Region 1]
    kafka --> replica2[Replica Region 2]
    kafka --> replica3[Replica Region 3]
    decision --> fallback[Fallback Handler]
    fallback --> engine
```

### Step‑by‑Step Flow

1. **Request Intake** – A write request originates from a client and is received by the API gateway. The gateway forwards the payload to the ACE without altering the semantics of the operation.

2. **Decision Service** – The ACE forwards the request to the Decision Service, which consults the in‑memory policy cache. The cache is populated by the `PolicyReconciler`, a component that ingests policy changes from the central YAML store and emits a *PolicyChanged* event into the Axon event store.

3. **Policy Reconciliation** – The `PolicyReconciler` evaluates the request’s entity pattern against the active policy set. If a match is found, the required consistency level is attached to the request context. Otherwise, a default level (typically EVENTUAL) is applied.

4. **Event Sourcing** – The Decision Service writes a *WriteIntent* event to the Axon event store. Axon guarantees deterministic ordering and durability. The event contains the desired consistency level, the target entity, and the payload.

5. **Kafka Propagation** – A Kafka producer, wrapped with Resilience4j’s circuit breaker, publishes the *WriteIntent* to a dedicated topic. The producer’s fallback logic routes the event back to the Decision Service if the broker is unreachable, ensuring no data loss.

6. **Regional Replication** – Each regional replica consumes the topic. The consumer applies the consistency semantics encoded in the event:  
   * **STRONG** – The replica blocks further reads until it acknowledges the write.  
   * **EVENTUAL** – The replica updates its local store immediately and propagates the change asynchronously.  
   * **PROXIMITY** – The replica serves reads locally while queuing the write for eventual global consistency.

7. **Fallback Path** – If the Decision Service cannot resolve a policy (e.g., due to a transient cache miss), the Fallback Handler injects a conservative default (STRONG) and retries the event emission. This guarantees that critical writes never slip through with an unintended weaker guarantee.

### Consistency‑Level Trade‑Offs

| Consistency Level | Guarantee | Typical Latency | Use Cases |
|-------------------|-----------|-----------------|-----------|
| STRONG | Linearizable across all replicas | <10 ms | Financial transfers, GDPR‑sensitive writes |
| EVENTUAL | Last‑write‑wins, may read stale data | <1 ms | Analytics, user profiles |
| PROXIMITY | Reads from nearest replica, writes to all | <5 ms | Global e‑commerce catalog |
| CUSTOM | Application‑defined window | Variable | Time‑to‑live caches, session data |

The diagram and table together illustrate how ACE dynamically routes requests through a fault‑aware, event‑driven pipeline that respects declarative policies while preserving low latency for the majority of operations. By centralising decision logic, the engine eliminates duplicated consistency checks across services, reduces operational complexity, and provides a single source of truth for compliance enforcement.

## 📈 Operational Considerations & Monitoring

Transitioning from the policy‑driven routing logic introduced earlier, the Hybrid Consistency Engine (ACE) must be observable end‑to‑end so that operators can verify that adaptive guarantees are being honoured while the system remains resilient under load spikes, network partitions, or component failures. This section walks through the operational guardrails, the metric taxonomy, and the concrete steps required to instrument, alert, and act on the health of ACE in a production‑grade multi‑region deployment.

### 1. Deploy‑time Preconditions

| Pre‑deployment Check | Why It Matters | Recommended Tooling |
|----------------------|----------------|---------------------|
| Clock synchronization (NTP/Chrony) across all data‑center nodes | Strong‑eventual guarantees rely on deterministic timestamps for conflict resolution. | `chrony` with stratum‑1 sources, automated drift alerts |
| Uniform Resilience4j configuration version | Circuit‑breaker thresholds must be identical to avoid asymmetric throttling. | Spring Cloud Config + checksum validation |
| Axon Event Store schema version lock | Event replay consistency depends on a single source‑of‑truth for aggregate snapshots. | Flyway migration baseline, CI gate |

Failing any of these checks aborts the rollout and triggers a rollback to the last known‑good release.

### 2. Runtime Health‑Probes

ACE exposes three HTTP health endpoints that are polled by the orchestrator (Kubernetes, Nomad, etc.):

| Endpoint | Expected Semantics | Failure Action |
|----------|--------------------|----------------|
| `/health/readiness` | All required downstream brokers (Kafka, Axon) are reachable and the local event processor is subscribed. | Pod is removed from service mesh. |
| `/health/liveness` | JVM thread‑pool saturation < 80 % and no unhandled exceptions in the last 30 seconds. | Container restart. |
| `/health/consistency` | Current consistency policy cache hit‑rate > 95 % and no stale‑policy flag. | Alert escalated to SRE on‑call. |

Operators should configure the orchestrator to respect the hierarchy: liveness failures trigger immediate restarts, while readiness failures cause graceful traffic draining.

### 3. Metric Taxonomy

ACE’s instrumentation is split into three orthogonal dimensions: **Policy Engine**, **Event Pipeline**, and **Consistency Enforcement**. Each dimension publishes a set of Prometheus counters, gauges, and histograms that feed into Grafana dashboards.

| Dimension | Metric | Type | Normal Range | Alert Condition |
|-----------|--------|------|--------------|-----------------|
| Policy Engine | `ace.policy.cache.hit_rate` | gauge (0‑1) | > 0.95 | `< 0.90` for 5 min |
| Policy Engine | `ace.policy.update.latency_ms` | histogram | ≤ 50 ms p95 | `> 200 ms` p95 |
| Event Pipeline | `ace.kafka.producer.errors_total` | counter | 0 | `> 0` within 1 min |
| Event Pipeline | `ace.event.processor.duration_ms` | histogram | ≤ 30 ms p99 | `> 150 ms` p99 |
| Consistency Enforcement | `ace.consistency.switches_total` | counter | monotonic | sudden spike > ×5 baseline |
| Consistency Enforcement | `ace.strong_violation.count` | counter | 0 | `> 0` triggers immediate page |

The **Consistency Enforcement** counters are the most critical: a non‑zero `ace.strong_violation.count` indicates that a write intended to be strong was downgraded due to an unrecoverable fault, violating SLAs. Such an event must trigger a forced fail‑over and a manual audit.

### 4. Distributed Tracing Integration

Every request that traverses ACE is wrapped in a Spring Cloud Sleuth span. The trace propagates through:

1. **Ingress API Gateway** – entry span records the requested consistency level.
2. **Policy Decision Service** – child span logs policy lookup latency and fallback decisions.
3. **Event Publication** – another child span captures Kafka producer acknowledgments and retries.
4. **Replication Coordinator** – final span measures cross‑region commit latency.

By correlating spans with the `ace.consistency.switches_total` metric, operators can pinpoint whether a downgrade originated from a network partition, a circuit‑breaker open state, or a policy cache miss. The tracing UI should expose a “Consistency Flow” view that highlights any span where the `consistency` tag deviates from the inbound request.

### 5. Adaptive Scaling Rules

ACE leverages Spring Cloud LoadBalancer together with a custom Resilience4j bulkhead that caps concurrent event processors per region. The scaling policy is:

- **Scale‑out** when `ace.event.processor.duration_ms` p99 exceeds 100 ms for two consecutive minutes.
- **Scale‑in** when the same histogram remains under 30 ms for five minutes and the bulkhead utilization falls below 30 %.

These rules are encoded in the Kubernetes Horizontal Pod Autoscaler (HPA) using custom metrics adapters, ensuring that the system automatically provisions additional processors during flash crowds without sacrificing the strong‑eventual guarantees.

### 6. Incident Playbook Highlights

| Symptom | Immediate Check | Remediation |
|---------|----------------|-------------|
| Sudden rise in `ace.kafka.producer.errors_total` | Verify broker quorum and network latency. | Restart affected producer pods; if persistent, trigger broker fail‑over. |
| `ace.strong_violation.count` > 0 | Inspect circuit‑breaker state (`ace.cb.open_total`). | Force circuit‑breaker reset, validate downstream replica health, and re‑run failed writes from the dead‑letter queue. |
| Policy cache hit‑rate drops below 0.85 | Confirm Config Server availability. | Flush local cache, force a full policy reload, and monitor for repeat misses. |

The playbook should be version‑controlled alongside the codebase so that any change to the policy engine or event topology automatically updates the operational runbooks.

### 7. Continuous Improvement Loop

Finally, ACE’s observability stack feeds back into the development cycle. Every quarterly review extracts the top‑5 latency contributors from the `ace.event.processor.duration_ms` histogram, the top‑3 policy fallback reasons, and any non‑zero `ace.strong_violation.count`. These insights drive:

- Refactoring of hot‑path policy rules.
- Tuning of Resilience4j thresholds (failure‑rate, slow‑call‑duration).
- Adjustments to the replication quorum size for specific data‑domains.

By embedding this feedback loop, the Hybrid Consistency Engine not only guarantees adaptive strong‑eventual semantics but also evolves its operational posture to meet ever‑changing latency and reliability expectations.

## 🛠️ Open Source Implementations

The Hybrid Consistency Engine (HCE) is an architectural pattern that can be realized with a combination of mature open‑source components. Below is a concrete, production‑ready stack that demonstrates how the pattern can be assembled in a Java ecosystem, along with a concise configuration snippet, a high‑level topology diagram, and a flow diagram that illustrates the write‑through path.

### Core OSS Building Blocks

| Component | Language | Consistency Contribution | Integration |
|-----------|----------|--------------------------|-------------|
| Spring Cloud Gateway | Java | API routing + request throttling | Exposes the HCE REST surface |
| Spring Boot + Spring MVC | Java | Domain model + REST controller | Hosts the `HybridConsistencyController` |
| Axon Framework | Java | Event sourcing + CQRS | Captures domain events and drives eventual consistency |
| Resilience4j | Java | Circuit‑breaker + bulkhead | Protects the policy engine and event bus |
| Apache Kafka | Java | Distributed log | Provides durable, ordered event streams between regions |
| Spring Kafka | Java | Kafka client integration | Publishes and consumes domain events |
| PostgreSQL | SQL | Strongly‑consistent write store | Persists the authoritative state in the primary region |
| Hazelcast / In‑Memory Cache | Java | Fast read cache | Serves read‑through requests with optional read‑repair |

The stack is intentionally minimal: it avoids external coordination services (e.g., ZooKeeper) by leveraging Kafka’s built‑in leader election and the resilience primitives of Resilience4j. The policy engine, written as a Spring bean, evaluates the *strong‑eventual* contract on each write and decides whether to trigger a synchronous replication or to enqueue a delayed consistency job.

### Java Domain Model & REST Endpoint

The domain model is expressed through a set of interfaces and abstract classes that capture the *strong* and *eventual* facets:

```java
public interface StrongEventualEntity {
    UUID getId();
    long getVersion();
}

public abstract class AbstractEntity implements StrongEventualEntity {
    protected UUID id;
    protected long version;
    // getters / setters
}
```

A REST controller exposes a single endpoint that accepts a `CreateOrderCommand`. The controller delegates to the Axon `CommandGateway`, which in turn triggers the policy engine:

```java
@RestController
@RequestMapping("/orders")
public class OrderController {
    private final CommandGateway gateway;

    public OrderController(CommandGateway gateway) {
        this.gateway = gateway;
    }

    @PostMapping
    public CompletableFuture<UUID> create(@RequestBody CreateOrderCommand cmd) {
        return gateway.send(cmd);
    }
}
```

The policy engine is wired through Spring configuration:

```java
@Configuration
public class HceConfig {

    @Bean
    public CircuitBreakerRegistry circuitBreakerRegistry() {
        return CircuitBreakerRegistry.ofDefaults();
    }

    @Bean
    public KafkaTemplate<String, DomainEvent> kafkaTemplate() {
        return new KafkaTemplate<>(producerFactory());
    }

    @Bean
    public PolicyEngine policyEngine(CircuitBreakerRegistry registry,
                                     KafkaTemplate<String, DomainEvent> kafka) {
        return new PolicyEngine(registry, kafka);
    }
}
```

